    "17592186405204": {
      "course/code": "MTH 255",
      "course/description": "Vector Calculus II develops the calculus of vector fields and integration along curves and over surfaces. Building on Vector Calculus I, the course connects local descriptions of a field with accumulated quantities such as work, circulation, and flux. Its central tools include conservative fields, divergence, curl, and the integral theorems of Gauss and Stokes, with applications involving force, work, mass, and charge.",
      "course/id": {
        "$uuid": "c37c596e-77c4-5554-8295-443fb5da076b"
      },
      "course/outcomes": [
        17592186406505,
        17592186406506,
        17592186406507,
        17592186406508,
        17592186406509,
        17592186406510,
        17592186406511,
        17592186406512,
        17592186406513,
        17592186406514
      ],
      "course/overview": "The course revisits vector functions, space curves, gradients, and directional derivatives before extending integration to curves and surfaces. Line integrals connect vector fields with work and circulation. Conservative fields, potential functions, and path independence explain when an integral can be determined from endpoint information rather than the details of a path.\n\nSurface geometry and oriented surface elements provide the foundation for surface integrals and flux. Divergence and curl describe local field behavior, while the Divergence Theorem and Stokes’ Theorem connect these local quantities to integrals over regions and their boundaries. The archived course materials emphasize geometric understanding and the use of vector differentials to connect the various integration methods.",
      "course/school": "Oregon State University",
      "course/title": "Vector Calculus II",
      "db/id": 17592186405204
    },
